 ## Overview
-Search Data Structure Based Isosurface Extraction is a family of methods that utilize external search structures (indices) to accelerate isosurface extraction for multiple queries on the same dataset [S1]. By creating an index that allows the algorithm to disregard regions containing only non-active cells, these methods achieve significant performance gains over brute-force traversal when repeated queries are required [S1]. This approach is distinct from single-pass methods because the overhead of index construction must be amortized over multiple queries; it is not feasible if the dataset is examined only once or if the index transfer overhead is prohibitive [S1].
+Search Data Structure Based Isosurface Extraction is a family of methods that utilize external search structures (indices) to accelerate isosurface extraction for multiple queries on the same dataset [S1]. By creating an index that allows the algorithm to disregard regions containing only non-active cells, these methods achieve significant performance gains over brute-force traversal when repeated queries are required [S1]. This approach is distinct from single-pass methods like [Fixed-Sized Buckets for Isosurface Extraction](1252-fixed-sized-buckets-for-isosurface-extraction.md) and [Interval Table for Isosurface Extraction](4357-interval-table-for-isosurface-extraction.md) because the overhead of index construction must be amortized over multiple queries; it is not feasible if the dataset is examined only once or if the index transfer overhead is prohibitive [S1].
 
 ## Shared principles
-The core principle of this family is the pre-computation of a spatial or value-based index to filter out inactive cells during the query phase [S1].
-
-**Index Construction:**
-The dataset is analyzed to build a data structure that maps spatial locations or value ranges to the cells within the volume [S1]. The structure must support efficient range queries or spatial lookups to identify which cells intersect with a specific isovalue $q$ [S1].
-
-**Query Mechanism:**
-For a given isovalue $q$, the search structure is queried to enumerate only the "active" cells $c$ where the isosurface might exist [S1]. Cells that are definitively inactive (where $q$ falls outside the cell's value range or spatial bounds) are skipped [S1].
-
-**Performance Trade-off:**
-The total execution time is the sum of index creation time and extraction time [S1]. This method is only beneficial when:
-1. Multiple isosurfaces or isovalue queries are performed on the same static dataset [S1].
-2. The dataset is large enough that skipping non-active cells saves more time than the index lookup overhead [G].
-
-**Limitations:**
-The method is not feasible if:
-1. The dataset is examined only once (e.g., medical examination results, CFD simulation snapshots) [S1].
-2. The data must be transferred to another resource, as the download overhead of the index may exceed the computation savings [S1].
+The core mechanism common to all methods in this family is the pre-computation of an external search structure to filter out inactive cells during query time [S1]. The dataset is indexed so that a query for a specific isovalue $q$ can efficiently enumerate only the active cells $c$ where the isosurface might exist, skipping regions that contain exclusively non-active cells [S1]. The total execution time is the sum of the index creation time and the extraction time [S1]. This method is only beneficial when multiple isosurfaces or isovalue queries are performed on the same static dataset, as the index construction overhead must be amortized [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Range-based Techniques
-Cells (or fixed-size groups of cells) are associated with a continuous value range $R(c) = [\min_{p \in C} f(p), \max_{p \in C} f(p)]$, where $f(p)$ is the scalar field value at vertex $p$ [S1]. A query for isovalue $q$ enumerates all cells $c$ such that $q \in R(c)$ [S1]. Common structures include kd-trees and interval trees [S1]. See [Standard Interval Tree for Isosurface Extraction](1230-standard-interval-tree-for-isosurface-extraction.md) and [Fixed-Sized Buckets for Isosurface Extraction](1252-fixed-sized-buckets-for-isosurface-extraction.md).
